<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Mahra Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1432 CE – 1967 CE (approx.) · capital Tamrida</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The sultanate of the Mahra people, ruling the far east of Yemen and the island of Socotra until the British withdrawal in 1967.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Mahra, a people speaking a Modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabian</a> language rather than Arabic, lived in the far east of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Yemen</a> and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socotra">Socotra</a>. In the 15th century the Afrar family established a sultanate ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qishn">Qishn</a> on the mainland and the island of Socotra, with a seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadibu">Tamrida</a> (modern Hadibu).</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultanate was loose, depending on tribal loyalty and revenues from frankincense, aloes, livestock and ship traffic. In 1507 the Portuguese seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socotra">Socotra</a> and held it until 1511. In 1886 the sultan signed a protection treaty with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a>, and the sultanate became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aden-protectorate">Aden Protectorate</a>, with Socotra valued for its position on shipping routes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehri-language">Mehri</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soqotri-language">Soqotri</a> are still spoken.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a> took over the region in 1967, the sultanate was abolished and incorporated into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/south-yemen">South Yemen</a>, later the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Republic of Yemen</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mahra-sultanate.jpg" alt="Mahra Sultanate" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Ivan Sache · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Mahra_Sultanate.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1432 CE – 1967 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tamrida</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mehri, Soqotri, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mahra Sultanate of Qishn and Socotra, Sultanate of Qishn and Socotra, Mahra</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1700&amp;focus=mahra-sultanate" aria-label="Mahra Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6091 1590 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mahra Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6411 1846L6295 1853M6411 1846L6236 1957"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6295 1853h0M6236 1957h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6411 1846h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6411 1846h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1700&amp;focus=mahra-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
